# Terraform — state can contain secrets (kubeconfig material, Grafana password)
**/.terraform/
# NB: .terraform.lock.hcl is deliberately NOT ignored. HashiCorp's guidance is to
# commit it: it pins provider versions and checksums, so ~> constraints resolve to
# the same builds on every machine and in CI. Ignoring it silently reintroduces the
# drift the constraints exist to prevent.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
crash.log
crash.*.log
# Saved plans. NOT covered by *.tfstate, and just as sensitive: a plan file is an
# unencrypted archive of the whole resource graph, including values Terraform marks
# sensitive in console output. `terraform show` reads one back in plain text.
*.tfplan
*.tfplan.*
tfplan
# tfvars often hold project IDs / account-specific values — commit only the .example
*.tfvars
!*.tfvars.example
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json
.terraformrc
terraform.rc

# Taskfile dotenv — holds GCP_PROJECT / regions, i.e. account-specific values
.env

# Local kube/helm artefacts
kubeconfig
*.kubeconfig

# Python. scripts/llm-sim.py is stdlib-only and runs locally via `make selftest`,
# which leaves bytecode next to the source. (The in-cluster copy sets
# PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 because its root filesystem is read-only.)
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/

# Agent/editor local state. settings.local.json is per-machine and holds tool
# permission allowlists; a shared .claude/settings.json is fine to commit, so this
# targets only the local variant rather than the whole directory.
**/.claude/settings.local.json

# The authoring briefs, kept locally but deliberately untracked. Globbed rather than
# listed one by one — the list went stale the first time a new brief was written.
prompt.md
prompt-*.md

# ⚠️ CLAUDE.md IS TRACKED, and the distinction is the point. A brief is a one-off
# specification for a single piece of work; CLAUDE.md is the repo's standing law —
# the invariants, the working loop, the traps — and the live briefs now CITE it by
# rule number instead of restating it, so a clone without it loses the half of each
# brief that was deleted as duplication.
#
# The negation is documentation first and a guard second: nothing above matches
# CLAUDE.md today, so it changes no current behaviour. It sits here because this block
# is directly under the `.claude/` rule, and a tightening written as `.claude*` or
# `CLAUDE*` in THIS block would otherwise swallow the file silently — it would simply
# stop arriving in clones, with nothing to notice.
#
# ⚠️ It protects against a hostile rule ABOVE it and NOT below it — git takes the LAST
# matching pattern, so a `CLAUDE*` appended to the end of this file still wins.
# Verified both ways, 2026-08-03. If you add a broad markdown or CLAUDE rule later,
# re-assert this negation beneath it.
!CLAUDE.md

# Unwrapped from the Kubernetes manifests by compose/compose.yaml at start-up. Never
# edited by hand and never committed: manifests/ stays the single source of truth.
compose/.generated/

# Derived from manifests/dashboards/*.json by scripts/dashboard-publish.py for the
# grafana.com upload. Same rule as above — committing it would put a second copy of
# each board in the repo, which is the drift the single-artefact design avoids.
dist/

# Raw screen captures. `demo.mp4` IS tracked — it is the walkthrough the README links, and
# at ~6 MB it is a reasonable thing to clone. The master it was encoded from is not: a
# retina `Cmd+Shift+5` capture of a six-minute run is ~74 MB, and git keeps it forever even
# after a later commit deletes it. Ignoring the CONTAINER rather than a filename, because
# the name is whatever the recorder chose that day; a `.mov` here is always a capture.
*.mov

# OS / editor cruft
.DS_Store
*.swp
*.swo
.idea/
.vscode/
